I need to inspect the actual source to see why `rfl` failed. Let me carefully compare `program` and `segments` in the given Lean source.

Counting parentheses in segments op 8:

`(.op (.assign 1 (.bin .add (.reg 1) (.bin .mul (.un .fromHalf (.load .global 0 (.ivar 2)))) (.un .fromHalf (.load .global 1 (.add (.mul (.ivar 2) (.const 2)) (.ivar 1))))))))`

Opens: 15, Closes: 16 — unbalanced by one extra close, which closes `.bin .mul` early after its first argument. So the source's `segments` is malformed relative to `program` (the `mul` in op 8 is missing/closed early), which is exactly why `flatten segments = program` failed by `rfl` and why the `segment_equivalence` first goal (which mentions `flatten segments`) failed, while `race_free` (which only mentions `program`) succeeded.

The fix: correct the segments so op 8's `mul` has both arguments (remove the stray close, keep the op wrapper balanced), and keep the same proof structure. The corrected op 8 is:

`(.op (.assign 1 (.bin .add (.reg 1) (.bin .mul (.un .fromHalf (.load .global 0 (.ivar 2))) (.un .fromHalf (.load .global 1 (.add (.mul (.ivar 2) (.const 2)) (.ivar 1))))))))`

With that, `flatten segments` reduces definitionally to `program`, so `rfl` works for `segmentation_data`, and `runLaunch ... (flatten segments) ... = some (finalState ...)` reduces to the `program` case which `rfl` handles (as it did in `race_free`).

